text:
jules: FYI customer on Vercel Edge (Next 14) hit a crash when they `require('redwood-inference')` from a Server Component: \"SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'export'\". They can't flip to ESM easily. anyone seen this pattern?
marta: seen similar. Usually bundler resolved our ESM build into the edge bundle even though consumer uses CJS. What's their package.json look like?
li: they sent this minimal pack: ```json
{
  "name": "legacy-app",
  "type": "commonjs",
  "dependencies": { "redwood-inference": "^1.2.0" }
}
```
jules: yep thats it. they do `const client = require('redwood-inference').default || require('redwood-inference')` and still blows up at runtime in the Edge.
akash: quick explainer: Node resolution + bundlers (Next/Vite) look at the `exports` field first. If our package `exports` exposes an `import` target but no explicit `require` subpath, some bundlers will prefer the ESM target for tree-shaking and end up bundling ESM source into a CJS consumer. That produces `export` tokens in the runtime where only CJS `require` is expected.
marta: short-term workaround for customer: add a top-level alias in their next config to force cjs entry: ```js
// next.config.js
module.exports = { webpack: (cfg) => { cfg.resolve.alias['redwood-inference'] = require.resolve('redwood-inference/dist/cjs'); return cfg; } }
``` but not ideal.
li: long-term fix: we should publish a clear CJS entry and explicit `exports` mapping including a `.require` path. e.g.:
```json
"exports": {
  ".": { "import": "./dist/esm/index.js", "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js" },
  "./stream": { "import": "./dist/esm/stream.js", "require": "./dist/cjs/stream.js" }
}
```
That prevents bundlers from accidentally grabbing ESM when the consumer expects CJS.
akash: also important: streaming surface compatibility. Edge expects web streams (ReadableStream). Our CJS runtime currently returns Node streams for streamed responses which breaks in edge. The CJS wrapper should detect environment and export a web-stream friendly shape. Example wrapper:```js
// dist/cjs/index.js
const isWeb = typeof ReadableStream !== 'undefined' || process.env.REDWOOD_FORCE_WEB === '1';
module.exports = isWeb ? require('../esm/index.js') : require('./runtime-node.js');
```
This keeps one implementation (esm) for web-friendly runtimes while preserving node-specific runtime in `runtime-node.js`.
marta: perfect. can we also add a small integration test that simulates the Vercel Edge runtime by setting global.ReadableStream and running the streaming smoke?
li: adding that to the PR. Also adding a note to RELEASE_NOTES about the new `dist/cjs` entry.
jules: could we publish a `canary-cjs` tag so customers can test quickly?
akash: yes. I'll cut a canary release from branch `pr-315-cjs-entry` and tag `canary-cjs`. Use `npm i redwood-inference@canary-cjs` or `npm i github:redwood-inference/sdk#pr-315` for verification.
